.TH SHARDPOSTCTL-MAILBOX 1 "2026-09-20" "ShardPost 0.26.1" "ShardPost Manual"
.SH NAME
shardpostctl-mailbox \- list, inspect, create, rename and subscribe mailboxes
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B shardpostctl mailbox list
.RB [ \-all ]
.RB [ \-limit
.IR n ]
.RB [ \-after
.IR name ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.I account
.br
.B shardpostctl mailbox show
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.I account mailbox
.br
.B shardpostctl mailbox create
.RB [ \-special-use
.IR list ]
.I account name
.br
.B shardpostctl mailbox rename
.RB [ \-if-match
.IR etag ]
.I account mailbox "new name"
.br
.B shardpostctl mailbox subscribe
.RB [ \-if-match
.IR etag ]
.I account mailbox
.br
.B shardpostctl mailbox unsubscribe
.RB [ \-if-match
.IR etag ]
.I account mailbox
.SH DESCRIPTION
A
.I mailbox
argument is either the mailbox name as the client sees it
.RI ( INBOX ", " Archive/2026 )
or its stable id. The client resolves a name to its id first, so a
mailbox that is renamed between two commands is still the same object.
.SS mailbox list
Mailboxes in name order with id, UIDVALIDITY, UIDNEXT, HIGHESTMODSEQ,
subscription, special-use and the number of ACL entries. One page holds
.B \-limit
entries (default 100);
.B \-after
continues after a name, and
.B \-all
fetches every page. This command reads the registry and one header per
mailbox, so it stays cheap even for large accounts.
.PP
Role:
.BR diagnose .
.SS mailbox show
One mailbox in detail. On top of the listing it reports the message
count, how many are unseen and flagged deleted, the total size, the
number of index shards and their size, where the change log starts (the
QRESYNC horizon) and the keywords in use. It reads every index shard of
the mailbox, so it costs more than
.BR "mailbox list" .
.PP
The shard line is worth knowing:
.I "shards 47 of 20 UIDs"
means the mailbox was created when
.I mbox_shard_size
was 20. The size is fixed when the mailbox is created and never changes
afterwards; only a rebuild (export, purge, restore) moves an existing
mailbox to the current size.
.PP
Role:
.BR diagnose .
.SS mailbox create
Creates a mailbox, with parents as needed.
.B \-special-use
takes a comma-separated list
.RI ( \e\fBArchive\fP ", " \e\fBSent\fP ", " \e\fBJunk\fP ", " \e\fBTrash\fP ", "
.IR \e\fBDrafts\fP ", " \e\fBAll\fP ", " \e\fBFlagged\fP ", " \e\fBImportant\fP );
it is stored in the usual spelling. If the mailbox already exists with
the same special-use, the command succeeds and changes nothing, so a
repeated run after a lost answer is safe. With a different special-use it
is a conflict.
.PP
Role:
.BR provision .
.SS mailbox rename
Renames a mailbox together with its children. INBOX cannot be renamed,
and an existing target is a conflict. Renaming to the current name does
nothing.
.PP
Role:
.BR provision .
.SS mailbox subscribe, mailbox unsubscribe
Sets or clears the subscription flag that clients use to decide which
mailboxes to show.
.PP
Role:
.BR provision .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-limit " n"
Mailboxes per page (mailbox list), 1 to 500.
.TP
.BI \-after " name"
Continue the listing after this mailbox name.
.TP
.B \-all
Fetch every page.
.TP
.BI \-special-use " list"
Special-use attributes of a new mailbox, comma-separated.
.TP
.BI \-if-match " etag"
Make the change conditional. The ETag is printed by every command that
changes a mailbox; if the mailbox changed meanwhile, the server refuses
with exit code 65 instead of overwriting someone else's change.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print the answer as JSON.
.SH EXAMPLES
Public folders are administered like an account, with
.B ~public
as the account. They belong to nobody, so nobody has rights on them
until an entry grants some; see
.BR shardpostctl-acl (1).
.PP
.nf
.RS
shardpostctl mailbox create '~public' Announcements
shardpostctl acl set '~public' Announcements anyone lr
shardpostctl mailbox list -all '~public'
.RE
.fi
.PP
Which mailboxes does the account have, and how big is the INBOX?
.PP
.nf
.RS
shardpostctl mailbox list -all user@example.org
shardpostctl mailbox show user@example.org INBOX
.RE
.fi
.PP
Create an archive mailbox for a user who asked for one:
.PP
.nf
.RS
shardpostctl mailbox create -special-use '\eArchive' \\
    user@example.org Archive
.RE
.fi
.PP
Rename a mailbox, but only if nobody else changed it since you looked:
.PP
.nf
.RS
shardpostctl mailbox show user@example.org Projekte      # prints the etag
shardpostctl mailbox rename -if-match '"m-0808d3b73bb67293"' \\
    user@example.org Projekte Projects
.RE
.fi
.PP
Make a mailbox visible in the client:
.PP
.nf
.RS
shardpostctl mailbox subscribe user@example.org Projects
.RE
.fi
.PP
Deleting a mailbox is not done here. It is a planned job, so you can see
what would be removed before anything happens; see
.BR shardpostctl-job (1):
.PP
.nf
.RS
shardpostctl plan -mailbox 'Archive/2019' mailbox_delete user@example.org
.RE
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Done.
.TP
.B 65
Conflict, or the ETag no longer matches.
.TP
.B 67
No such account or mailbox.
.TP
.B 77
The caller lacks the role.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR shardpostctl (1),
.BR shardpostctl-acl (1),
.BR shardpostctl-job (1),
.BR shardpostctl-message (1)
